      "problem": "${log}_{4}⁡64=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite $64$ as a power of $4$ (the base of the logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n64={4}^{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n{log}_{4}⁡64={log}_{4}⁡({4}^{3})\n$$\n\nThe logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n{log}_{b}⁡({b}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $b=4$ and $x=3.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n{log}_{4}⁡({4}^{3})=3\n$$",
